To find all the roots of a polynomial to the n-th degree using Newton's method, you can use the method of successive approximations. This involves finding a first approximation to the root, then using this approximation to find a second approximation, and so on until the desired level of accuracy is reached.

To find the first approximation, you can use the multi-point formula:
x_0 = (b_0/b_1)
x_1 = (b_1/b_2)
...
x_n = (b_n/b_(n-1))

where b_i are the coefficients of the polynomial and x_i are the values of the polynomial at the points i.

Once you have the first approximation, you can use the Newton's method to find the second approximation, and so on:
x_{k+1} = x_k - \frac{f(x_k)}{f'(x_k)}

where f(x) is the polynomial and f'(x) is its derivative.

To find the roots of the polynomial, you can repeat this process until the desired level of accuracy is reached. A good measure of accuracy is the root mean square error (RMSE):
RMSE = sqrt(sum((x_k - x_k+1)^2))

where k is the current iteration and x_k is the value of the polynomial at the previous iteration.

To find the roots of the polynomial, you can set the desired criterion for the RMSE to a small value, such as 10^-6, and iterate until the RMSE is less than this value.<|end_of_text|>

